course: adult-life
lesson: 44
title: The royal family in the news: a wedding, a move to America, an interview and a book
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The British royal family is the most gossiped-about family in the world. Their weddings, babies and funerals are public events shown on television, but they are also a real family with real feelings, quarrels and sadness, like any other.
explain: Royal news comes in layers. At the bottom are the facts: dates, official statements from the Palace, things filmed for everyone to see. Above them are «royal sources», newspaper columns and online guesses — some right, many wrong, none of them checkable.
explain: Scene: in Glasgow in 2018, Nan is glued to the telly: Prince Harry is marrying Meghan Markle, an American actress, at St George's Chapel in Windsor. The wedding is a fact the whole world watched. What the guests «really» thought, and the rows over the plans, were newspaper stories.
explain: When someone tells their own side, in an interview, a TV series or a book, that is one honest side, not the whole story. The others in it may have said nothing in public, and a fair person remembers that.
explain: A good rule for any famous family: say what was officially announced or widely shown, call guesses guesses, and don't fill the blanks with your own story.
fact: Prince Harry married Meghan Markle at St George's Chapel, Windsor, on 19 May 2018, and they became the Duke and Duchess of Sussex.
fact: Meghan's father did not come to the wedding; it was reported he had heart surgery, and Charles, then Prince of Wales, walked her part of the way.
fact: In January 2020 Harry and Meghan announced they would step back as senior working royals, and they later moved to California; Harry is still a prince.
fact: In March 2021 they gave a TV interview to Oprah Winfrey and spoke of a hurtful remark by a family member they did not name; the Palace replied that «some recollections may vary» and that the family would deal with it privately.
fact: In February 2021 the Palace confirmed they would not return as working royals, and Harry's special army titles went back to the Queen.
fact: Prince Philip died on 9 April 2021 aged 99, and the Queen sat alone at his funeral because of Covid distancing rules.
fact: Queen Elizabeth II died on 8 September 2022 at Balmoral in Scotland, aged 96, after seventy years as Queen.
fact: Charles became King the moment his mother died and was crowned with Queen Camilla at Westminster Abbey on 6 May 2023, which was also Harry's son Archie's birthday; Harry came, and Meghan stayed in California with the children.
fact: In December 2022 a Netflix series told Harry and Meghan's side of their story; others in it did not reply on camera.
fact: Harry's memoir «Spare» came out on 10 January 2023 and was reported as the fastest-selling non-fiction book in UK history.
fact: In «Spare» Harry wrote that William once pushed him to the floor in an argument; William has not told his side in public.
fact: In November 2019 Prince Andrew stepped back from public duties after a BBC interview about his friendship with a man convicted of serious crimes.
fact: A headline word like «Megxit» or «snub» makes a complicated choice sound simple and nasty.
fact: A few seconds of video of two brothers walking together does not tell you whether they have made up.
fact: Serious accusations belong to courts and investigations, not to jokes in the playground.
exercise: Nan, 2018: «Prince Harry's marrying an American actress, at Windsor!» Where was the wedding? | St George's Chapel, Windsor | Westminster Abbey, London | Balmoral Castle, Scotland | A registry office in California
exercise: Auntie Bev: «Meghan's dad didn't walk her down the aisle, there was a whole drama!» What's confirmed? | He didn't come, and Charles walked her part of the way | He refused because he hated the royal family and everything about it | He came but was kept out by the guards | He walked her down the aisle after all
exercise: Mum, 2019: «Baby Archie, and no photo on the hospital steps — people are FURIOUS!» Who were the «furious» people mostly? | Newspaper columns | The Queen and the King | The hospital staff | The baby's grandparents
exercise: Dad, 2020: «Harry and Meghan are quitting! Megxit!» What did they actually announce? | They'd step back as senior working royals | They'd stop being part of the family | Harry would give up being a prince for good | They'd never visit Britain again
exercise: Nan: «That Oprah interview! Who said that thing about the baby's skin? I bet it was…» What's the fair answer? | They didn't name the person, so we don't know | It was definitely the Queen herself | It was definitely Prince William, his brother | The newspapers found out who it was
exercise: Auntie Jen: «'Recollections may vary' is posh for 'you're lying'!» What did the statement actually say? | Issues were concerning, would be dealt with privately | Harry and Meghan were lying about everything they said | The family would sue them in court | The Queen would never speak to them again
exercise: Mum, 2021: «No more military titles for Harry!» What's the official fact? | The Palace said they wouldn't return as working royals | The Queen punished Harry for the interview by sending him away | Harry was thrown out of the army in disgrace | Harry asked to lose his military titles
exercise: Grandad: «The Queen sat all alone at Philip's funeral.» Why? | Covid rules on distancing at the time | The family had a big row that day | She asked to be left alone at the funeral | Nobody else was allowed in the chapel
exercise: Big sister: «Harry and William walked together after the funeral, so they've made up!» What's fair to say? | They walked and talked; nobody said they made up | They definitely made up that afternoon | They had a fight right after the walk | They were told by the palace to walk together for the cameras
exercise: Mum: «They called her Lilibet — the Queen's family nickname!» What's a fact and what isn't? | The nickname is fact; whether the Queen minded is disputed | Both the name and the Queen's anger are facts | The name was chosen by the Queen herself as a present for the baby | The baby was named after a famous actress
exercise: Mum, 8 September 2022: «The Queen's died. At Balmoral.» How old was she? | Ninety-six | Ninety-nine | One hundred | Eighty-nine
exercise: Grandad: «So Charles is king now, just like that.» When did he become King? | The moment his mother died | On his coronation day in 2023 | When Parliament voted for him | A month later, after the funeral
exercise: Big brother: «Harry, Meghan, William and Kate looked at the flowers together. Fab Four are back!» What's actually known? | The two couples walked and met people together | The brothers announced they had made up for good | The four of them moved back to Windsor | They argued while looking at the flowers
exercise: Auntie Jen: «Harry's book 'Spare' tells EVERYTHING.» What's fairer? | It's his memories, as he remembers them | It's the full, proved truth about the family | It was written by the whole royal family | It tells nothing that wasn't known already
exercise: Big brother: «In Spare, William punched Harry! Proper punch-up!» What did Harry actually write? | His brother pushed him to the floor in an argument | William punched him in the face many times | They had a fist fight right in front of the Queen at Balmoral | They threw chairs at each other at dinner
exercise: Mum, 2023: «They've been told to leave Frogmore Cottage.» What is confirmed? | They were asked to vacate it; they lived in the USA | They were thrown out of their only home in the world | The King moved into Frogmore himself | They sold Frogmore Cottage for money
exercise: Auntie Bev: «Harry flew straight back after the coronation. Says it all.» What reason was there? | It was his son Archie's birthday that day | He was banned from the coronation party | He had an argument with the King | He didn't like the music at the Abbey
exercise: Grandad: «Andrew did that BBC interview and then — gone.» What's the official fact? | He stepped back from public duties | He was sent to prison after it | He moved to America with Harry | He became King for one day
test: Nan: «The coronation! Harry came, Meghan stayed home. A SNUB!» What reason did their side give? | It was Archie's birthday, so she stayed with the children | Meghan was not invited to the Abbey at all | Meghan was ill and couldn't travel | Meghan wanted to watch it on the telly with her friends at home
test: Dad: «Netflix series, six episodes, about how hard it's been for them.» What should you remember when watching it? | It's their side; others didn't reply on camera | It's the full and checked truth from all sides | It was made by the Palace to explain things | It's a made-up story with actors
test: Your cousin texts: «my mum says the Queen sat alone cos nobody liked her lol». Put her right. | It was Covid distancing rules at the time | Yes, the family had fallen out with her | She wanted to sit alone to look sad | Prince Philip asked for her to sit alone at his funeral
test: Mum: «Andrew's in the paper again, all sorts of claims.» What is the right way to talk about it? | Say what was announced and leave the rest to courts | Repeat every claim from the paper as true, word for word | Make jokes about it, he's a prince | Guess what really happened and share it
test: Big brother: «'Megxit' means Meghan made Harry leave.» What's wrong with this? | It's a headline word, not the full story | Nothing, the word proves it was Meghan | It means Harry made Meghan leave instead | It's the Palace's official name for it
test: Auntie Jen, reading a magazine: «A guest said the bride was rude to the staff!» What kind of story is this? | A tabloid claim, not a confirmed fact | An official announcement from the Palace | Something everyone saw on television | A fact from Meghan's own statement
test: Grandad: «Philip was ninety-nine. And the Queen?» Mum: «Ninety-six, in 2022.» Which is true? | Philip died at 99 in 2021, the Queen at 96 in 2022 | Both died at 99 in the same year | The Queen died first, then Philip a year later | Philip died at 96, the Queen at 99
test: Nan: «William and Harry walked side by side, they're best mates again, I can tell.» What can a short clip really show? | That they walked and talked, no more | Everything they said to each other | That they will never argue again | That they had a fight off camera
test: Big sister: «So who did the Oprah comment, d'ya reckon? Let's guess on the group chat!» What's the decent thing? | Not guess; they never named the person | Guess, because it's just for fun | Ask the teacher to guess for the class | Vote on it and share the winner
test: Mum: «Spare's sold millions, the fastest non-fiction ever in the UK, they said.» Which word shows she's reporting? | «they said» | «Spare» | «millions» | «fastest»
test: Auntie Bev: «If Meghan's dad didn't come, she must have banned him.» Is that fair? | No, it was reported he had heart surgery | Yes, everyone knows she banned him from coming | Yes, the Palace said she banned him | No, he was busy at work that day
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the memoir «Spare»? | It constitutes the recollections of one participant in the events | It constitutes an official history issued by the Palace | It constitutes a jointly agreed account by the entire family | It constitutes a work of fiction featuring invented persons
exam: Identify the principal reason for which Queen Elizabeth II sat alone at the funeral of Prince Philip. | Regulations then in force concerning social distancing | A dispute among the members of the royal family | A request made by Prince Philip prior to his death | The absence of the remaining family from the service
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the arrangements announced in January 2020. | The Duke and Duchess of Sussex withdrew as senior working members of the family | The Duke of Sussex renounced his position in the line of succession to the throne | The Duke and Duchess of Sussex were expelled from the United Kingdom | The Duke of Sussex was deprived of the title of prince
exam: It is given that a newspaper attributes a remark to «a royal source». Determine the status of the remark. | It is an unattributed claim incapable of verification | It is an official statement issued by the Palace | It is a matter of record before the courts | It is the recorded testimony of the monarch
